Package: patrone-branding
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         adwaita-icon-theme,
         fonts-noto-core,
         fonts-patrone-sans,
         plymouth-theme-spinner,
         plymouth-theme-ubuntu-text
# Patrone's wallpapers in place of Ubuntu's: GNOME Shell depends on
# ubuntu-wallpapers (for its default wallpaper), so this package stands in
# for it, and apt removes Ubuntu's (Policy 7.6.2, replacing a package). The
# same for Help: GNOME's pages of GNOME Help depend on ubuntu-docs, Ubuntu's
# front page for them ("Ubuntu Desktop Guide", with Ubuntu's logo), and this
# package has GNOME's own front page (help/).
Provides: ubuntu-docs,
          ubuntu-wallpapers
Conflicts: ubuntu-docs,
           ubuntu-wallpapers,
           ubuntu-wallpapers-resolute
Replaces: ubuntu-docs,
          ubuntu-wallpapers,
          ubuntu-wallpapers-resolute
Description: Patrone Linux's name, logos and artwork
 Names the system Patrone Linux and puts Patrone's logo where Ubuntu's was:
 the boot splash, the boot menu, the login screen, Settings > About, the text
 console's banner and the welcome message at login, which also loses Ubuntu's
 help links, news and Ubuntu Pro advertising. The login screen gets Patrone's
 colors and a blue default avatar. Noto Sans is the typeface everywhere:
 the desktop, apps, the login screen, and web pages that ask for Segoe UI or
 a plain sans-serif font, Chromium's included. It installs the Patrone
 wallpaper as the default, light in the light style and dark in the dark
 one, with variants to choose from in Settings in place of Ubuntu's
 wallpapers (it replaces ubuntu-wallpapers), makes blue the accent color
 in place of Ubuntu's orange, gives the desktop, apps and the login screen
 Patrone's cursors (falling back to Adwaita's for any it lacks), turns
 Ubuntu's dock off by default, and sets up fastfetch with the Patrone logo.
 Help is GNOME Help, with GNOME's own front page in place of Ubuntu's (it
 replaces ubuntu-docs), and the logo icons apps ask for (start-here and
 distributor-logo) are Patrone's P in place of Ubuntu's, in every color of
 Yaru's icons. Removing the package puts Ubuntu's files back.
